<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asaf Jahi dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1724">1724</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Asaf Jahis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Asaf Jahi dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> State in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 1724 to 1948 under the title of Nizam. It was founded by the Mughal noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Mir Qamar-ud-din Khan</a>, Asaf Jah I, and its seven rulers became the foremost princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> after accepting a subsidiary alliance in 1798. The dynasty's rule ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> annexed Hyderabad in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asaf-jahi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Asaf Jahi dynasty" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nizam of Hyderabad, India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyderabad_Coat_of_Arms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
